with negative CFO, suggests the growth may be consuming cash rather than generating it, which is not reconciled with the demand narrative.","fit":"does not fit","evidence":["E31","E39","E25","E26","E32","E37"],"verdict":"explanation does not fit the numbers"}}],"rulings":[{"attrs":{"caseId":"1375365_2015-06-30","revisionId":"d0671290cb58","sampleIndex":1,"rulingIndex":3,"finding_index":3,"ruling":"fits","reason":"Management states growth is driven by end-market demand and monitors DSO/inventory (E32, E37). Reported revenue growth of 35.7% (E13 vs E1) and the disclosed inventory build to support anticipated growth (E39) corroborate the demand narrative. The Beneish M-score flag (E31) is a known statistical artifact in high-growth firms and does not contradict the reported demand drivers.","evidence":["E1","E13","E31","E32","E37","E39"],"rebuttal":{"finding_index":3,"innocent_explanation":"The Beneish M-score flag is a false positive common in high-growth firms; the elevated SGI stems from 35.7% revenue growth and the elevated DSRI from receivables growing faster due to a shift toward complete systems sold to internet data center customers with longer payment terms, not from aggressive revenue recognition.","evidence":["E31","E13","E1","E19","E7","E32","E41","E56","E39"],"support":"numbers"},"final_verdict":"explanation does not fit the numbers","confidence":0.8}}],"audit":[{"seq":31,"actor":"evidence-pack","summary":"evidence:ingest:1375365_2015-06-30:d0671290cb58:E31 (evidence)","kind":"upsertEntity"},{"seq":141,"actor":"evidence-pack","summary":"evidence:1375365_2015-06-30:d0671290cb58:E31 (evidence)","kind":"upsertEntity"},{"seq":142,"actor":"evidence-pack","summary":"filing:accn:0001445305-14-003958 -[supports]-> evidence:1375365_2015-06-30:d0671290cb58:E31","kind":"relate"},{"seq":143,"actor":"evidence-pack","summary":"filing:accn:0001628280-15-007025 -[supports]-> evidence:1375365_2015-06-30:d0671290cb58:E31","kind":"relate"},{"seq":283,"actor":"nvidia/nemotron